<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to BML as BlockML's human-authored format

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.Bml-->
<docs:Bml xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to BML as BlockML's human-authored format
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: BML as the human-authored representation and its relationship to the canonical BOM,
    grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.Introduction and
    org.blockml.bml.documentation.DocumentFormat. Body content is authored in a03 from those
    sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:Introduction" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — the BML-authoring versus BOM-canonical distinction.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:DocumentFormat" relation="Requires">
      Document root, Sub-Block, and embedded Block authoring format.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:CompilerArchitecture" relation="SeeAlso">
      BML to Parser to BOM pipeline context.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>BML: The Authoring Format</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="BML as the authoring surface">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>BML is the human- and editor-friendly format in which a BlockML model is authored;
the BOM is that same model's canonical, immutable, and lossless representation used
afterward for processing. BML stays the format a person or tool edits directly, and
the BOM preserves the authored model without silently discarding or reinterpreting
it.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="A domain-agnostic compiler">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>The compiler is domain-agnostic: it parses and validates BML into the canonical
BOM without any built-in knowledge of a particular domain, so domain-specific
meaning belongs only to the render targets that consume the BOM afterward. The
compiler's own understanding stops at BML and the BOM; it never encodes a domain's
own vocabulary.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Identifying a Block's document root">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A BML document has a single root element that is itself the Block being
defined: its element tag supplies the Block's identity, and the rest of the
document describes that Block's members. The root is the definition itself, not
a wrapper placed around a separate identity.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Two accepted root forms">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>BlockML currently accepts two forms for a document root: a preferred form
where the element tag itself names the Block, and a legacy wrapper form kept
valid during a long transition. Both forms remain valid side by side until the
legacy form is formally withdrawn.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:CodeBlock>
              <code><![CDATA[<MyBlock>
  <baseType>
    core:Block
  </baseType>
</MyBlock>]]></code>
            </dpage:CodeBlock>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>In the example, the element tag alone identifies the Block being defined,
while an explicit baseType names the definition it extends; the next chapter
covers how a namespace prefix may qualify that tag.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="What to carry into namespaces">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>After this page, readers should be able to name BML as the authoring format,
the BOM as its canonical counterpart, and recognize a Block document by its
root element.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="Authoring vs canonical: BML is what a person or tool edits directly; the BOM is the compiler's canonical, lossless counterpart." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="Domain-agnostic compiler: the compiler understands only BML and the BOM, never a domain's own vocabulary." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="Root identifies the Block: a document's root element is the Block being defined, not a wrapper placed around it." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with BML namespaces">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="BML Namespaces" summary="Learn xmlns declarations and the bml-namespace placement rule.">
                  <target>docs:BmlNamespaces</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:Bml>
